{"step_index":321,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-16T18:05:33Z","input_tokens":3470,"cache_read_tokens":217665,"output_tokens":2873,"content":"### 1. Code Audit of [`benchmark_suite.py`](file:///home/debian/bluectfd/blueagent/benchmark_suite.py) & Findings from the Previous Run\n\nInspecting [`benchmark_suite.py`](file:///home/debian/bluectfd/blueagent/benchmark_suite.py) alongside the execution log from the cancelled run revealed **4 important findings**:\n\n1. **No Incremental Checkpointing:**\n   * [`export_csv()`](file:///home/debian/bluectfd/blueagent/benchmark_suite.py#L367) and [`export_markdown_report()`](file:///home/debian/bluectfd/blueagent/benchmark_suite.py#L401) were only called at the very end of [`main()`](file:///home/debian/bluectfd/blueagent/benchmark_suite.py#L455-L456). Even though the previous run actually completed Test 1, Test 3, and 11 matrix tiers before being cancelled, nothing was flushed to disk after Test 1.\n2. **Test 3 / Matrix Redundancy & Key Mismatch:**\n   * [`run_test_3()`](file:///home/debian/bluectfd/blueagent/benchmark_suite.py#L186-L253) ran $M \\in \\{2, 5, 10, 15\\}$ with $N=2$, which is identical to the $N=2$ column of [`run_tiered_matrix()`](file:///home/debian/bluectfd/blueagent/benchmark_suite.py#L258-L365). It also saved `deploy_wall_time` instead of `deploy_wall_sec`, causing `-` in the Markdown table.\n3. **Orphaned Sandbox Resources on Error:**\n   * [`ensure_clean_environment()`](file:///home/debian/bluectfd/blueagent/benchmark_suite.py#L106-L116) only called the BlueAgent REST API (`DELETE /teams/{k}/machines` and `DELETE /teams/{k}`), which removes live `c-{k}-*` containers but **does not purge orphaned `verify-{k}-*` clones or `verify-{k}` OVN switches** if a verification fails mid-flight.\n4. **Actual System Limit Discovered at $M=15$:**\n   * During the $M=15$ burst in the log, 14 out of 15 teams succeeded (`200 OK`), while Team 12 returned `500 Internal Server Error`:\n     > `Failed to start device \"eth0\": Failed setting up OVN port: Insufficient dynamic addresses allocated`\n   * Because `verify-12-app1` and `verify-12` were left behind as orphans, the subsequent tier failed on Team 12 with `UNIQUE constraint failed: instances.name`. Cleaning `verify-*` resources between tiers fixes the cascading failure while preserving the real OVN contention data point for your thesis.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Implementation Plan for [`benchmark_suite.py`](file:///home/debian/bluectfd/blueagent/benchmark_suite.py)\n\nWe will update [`benchmark_suite.py`](file:///home/debian/bluectfd/blueagent/benchmark_suite.py) with the following improvements:\n\n1. **Incremental Checkpoint & Resume (`thesis_stress_test_state.json`):**\n   * After **every single tier**, the runner immediately updates the JSON state file, [`thesis_stress_test_results.csv`](file:///home/debian/bluectfd/blueagent/tests/thesis_stress_test_results.csv), and [`thesis_stress_test_report.md`](file:///home/debian/bluectfd/blueagent/tests/thesis_stress_test_report.md).\n   * Running separate stages appends/updates the existing dataset rather than overwriting previous stages.\n2. **Unified Test 3 + Matrix Extraction:**\n   * The $N=2$ slice across $M \\in \\{2, 5, 10, 15\\}$ is executed once and automatically rendered in both the **Test 3 (Cross-Team Concurrency Burst)** section and the **Tiered Stress Matrix ($M \\times N$)** section of the thesis report.\n3. **Hardened Parallel Cleanup Between Tiers:**\n   * Pre-flight and post-tier cleanup runs `DELETE /teams/{k}/machines` and `DELETE /teams/{k}` in parallel via `asyncio.gather`.\n   * Also connects directly to `IncusEngine` (`/var/lib/incus/unix.socket`) to force-delete any lingering `verify-*` containers and `verify-*` OVN switches before starting the next tier.\n4. **Error & Pass-Ratio Tracking:**\n   * Records `passed_ratio` (e.g., `14/15`), HTTP error counts, and specific Incus/OVN error reasons per tier while computing latency percentiles (`p50`, `p95`, `avg`, `std`) across the successful requests.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Staged Execution Plan (Short Tests First)\n\nWe will execute the benchmark in **4 progressive stages**, verifying the output report after each stage:\n\n| Stage | Scope | Parameters | Peak Containers | Est. Duration | Goal |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Stage 1** | **Test 1: Baseline** | $M=1, N=1$ (10 cycles) | $2$ | **~45 sec** | Establish zero-contention latency floor & micro-phase breakdown |\n| **Stage 2** | **Lower Tiers (Quick Grid)** | $M \\in \\{2, 5\\} \\times N \\in \\{2, 5\\}$ | $8 \\to 50$ | **~2 min** | Validate fast multi-team & multi-container scaling |\n| **Stage 3** | **Mid Tiers** | $M \\in \\{2, 5\\} \\times N \\in \\{7, 10\\}$<br>$M=10 \\times N \\in \\{2, 5\\}$ | $28 \\to 100$ | **~4.5 min** | Measure linear container scaling ($N$) vs. team concurrency ($M=10$) |\n| **Stage 4** | **High-Stress Tiers** | $M=10 \\times N \\in \\{7, 10\\}$<br>$M=15 \\times N \\in \\{2, 5, 7, 10\\}$ | $140 \\to 300$ | **~8 min** | Probe the saturation knee, OVN port limits, and Incus UDS queueing |\n\nShall I update [`benchmark_suite.py`](file:///home/debian/bluectfd/blueagent/benchmark_suite.py) and run **Stage 1** and **Stage 2** now?","thinking":"User selected all proposed options. Need to understand the core goal: plan test implementation, but with an initial focus on questions and reviewing existing test code within the benchmark suite, favoring shorter tests initially.\n\n\nThe benchmark suite lacks incremental checkpointing, as report generation only occurs at the very end of the main function, which will be refactored with the user's choices in mind. Presenting a staged execution plan is the next step to address this; Stage 1 includes the baseline test, and Stage 2 incorporates a small grid. Refactoring `benchmark_suite.py` before executing these stages is under consideration.\n\n\nThe test suite loses completed tiers upon interruption and overwrites previous results during stage transitions, requiring a refactor for incremental progress. A key mismatch exists between time data keys used internally and by exporters in a specific test. The cleanup routine sequentially addresses live containers but neglects orphaned verification resources, potentially leading to leaks and failures. Real-world system limits surfaced when concurrent verifications reached a certain threshold, indicating a resource exhaustion issue with the underlying infrastructure.\n\n\nResource exhaustion is possible due to contention in dynamic address allocation during rapid network creation and deletion, particularly if verification networks are not cleaned up.  The benchmark suite refactor needs to implement incremental persistence after each tier and deduplicate test execution by merging Test 3 data into the Tiered Stress Matrix.  Parallel cleanup of challenge resources must be implemented.\n\n\n"}
